{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xj4GMgerTNBa1sHSN8uLzo","type":"tool_result","content":"\\midrule\naggregate & 0.0220 & 2.2\\% & 0.0129 & 0.0036 & 0.0091 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_vs_ppi_pp]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_linear_vs_ppi_pp]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Does the headroom analysis explain the gains?}\\label{sec:headroomcheck}\nWe evaluated Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling} on each pool, using $\\rho^2$ and an isotonic estimate of $\\eta^2$ on the full pool, $n=500$, $N=20000$, and the evaluator's PPI++ weight clip (for synthetic regimes, large-sample moments of the generator and the evaluator's episode plan). The population ceiling is not a bound on a single finite draw, and it excludes the estimation cost of $g$.\n\n\\emph{Where the ceiling is positive, it is small.} The ceiling against PPI++ is 0.0213 for census income, 0.0008 and 0.0009 for the two other census pools, and 0.0416 for the synthetic mixture; the aggregate is 0.0129. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_vs_ppi_pp]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_aggregate_vs_ppi_pp]]\nFor census income, the unclipped optimal weight computed from the pool is 1.34 (the clipped weight equals 1), and 0.0179 of the 0.0213 ceiling is reachable by an unclipped linear weight on $\\Yhat$ alone; the pool's $\\eta^2$ and $\\rho^2$ differ by only 0.0044. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_linear_vs_ppi_pp]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real.census_income]]\nSo for this family the predicted gain is mostly a consequence of the clip on the PPI++ weight, not of nonlinear recalibration. For the synthetic family, the entire ceiling comes from the nonlinear-miscalibrated regime ($\\eta^2=0.71$ against $\\rho^2=0.48$), and the observed 0.0413 matches the ceiling 0.0416 within 0.0003. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#synthetic_regimes]]\n\n\\emph{Where the observed gain exceeds the ceiling.} In four of five families the observed narrowing is larger than the ceiling (Table~\\ref{tab:final}). The plankton pool is the clearest case: the predictor takes only two values, so $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is affine in $\\Yhat$, the unclipped optimal weight (0.841) lies inside $[0,1]$, the ceiling is zero, yet the observed narrowing is 0.0359 (3.5\\%). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real.plankton]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_plankton]]\nThe aggregate observed value 0.0220 exceeds the aggregate ceiling 0.0129 by 0.0091. The same holds against PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ (0.0701 observed, 0.0661 ceiling) and against the labeled-only interval (0.3581 observed, 0.3498 ceiling). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_aggregate_vs_ppi_lam1]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_aggregate_vs_classical]]\nAsymptotic variance reduction from recalibration cannot account for this part. We did not run a diagnostic that isolates its source. Candidate explanations, none tested here, are (a) finite-sample optimism of an interval whose weight and residual variance are estimated on the same labeled data, (b) selection of the smaller of three estimated widths, which favors downward noise in the estimated standard error, and (c) differences in finite-sample variance estimation between the evaluated implementation and the reference baseline. Explanations (a) and (b) would also lower coverage and are consistent with the coverage shortfall of Section~\\ref{sec:validity}, but the available artifacts do not allow us to establish that link. For the two census pools with near-zero ceilings, the excess is 0.0009 and 0.0014; with no per-episode widths in the artifacts, we cannot tell whether it is distinguishable from pairing noise.\n\n\\emph{Regime-level headroom is much larger than the family-level average.} Within the nonlinear-miscalibrated synthetic regime alone, the same formula gives an oracle narrowing over an unclipped linear weight of 0.286 in log width for $(n,N)=(200,20000)$ and 0.178 for $(500,2000)$ (Table~\\ref{tab:rsens}), i.e.\\ roughly 25\\% to 16\\% shorter intervals; the gain falls as $r=n/N$ grows because the unlabeled sample then carries less of the variance. The family-level ceiling of 0.0416 is much smaller because three of the four regimes have no headroom and the evaluator's ratio of summed widths weights the nonlinear regime by its (smallest) interval width. For census income the same calculation gives at most 0.0034 over an unclipped linear weight, whatever $r$. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_recal_over_linear_by_cell]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Oracle recalibration gain over an unclipped linear weight on $\\hat Y$ (log width ratio, Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling}) by sample-size cell, for the one synthetic regime with nonlinear calibration and for the census-income pool (derived).}\n\\label{tab:rsens}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\n & $(200,20000)$ & $(500,20000)$ & $(200,2000)$ & $(500,2000)$ \\\\\n$r=n/N$ & 0.010 & 0.025 & 0.100 & 0.250 \\\\\n\\midrule\nsynthetic, nonlinear regime & 0.2855 & 0.2750 & 0.2323 & 0.1776 \\\\\ncensus income & 0.0034 & 0.0034 & 0.0030 & 0.0025 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_recal_over_linear_by_cell]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Stability across evaluator partitions}\nThe evaluator also reports the aggregate score separately for five partitions of the episodes, defined by the candidate reset seed: 0.0247, 0.0220, 0.0203, 0.0213 and 0.0235, all positive. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\nThese values are a spread, not a confidence interval: the data draws do not depend on the reset seed, and because of the evaluator's episode ordering each partition contains 76 episodes of one family and six of each other family, so partitions differ in composition (Supplement~\\ref{sec:si-partition}). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#partition_composition_main_episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Design-space contrast on the search pools}\\label{sec:search}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:search} compares the three model variants, each evaluated once on the search pools. Their aggregate scores against PPI++ are 0.0271 (recalibrated isotonic/binned), 0.0223 (stacked calibration) and 0.0046 (label-free basis with PRESS); all three were scored and passed the evaluator's validity gate. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_01]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_02]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_03]]\nThe recalibrated variant is largest on the synthetic family (0.0757) and gene expression (0.0290), and smallest on galaxy morphology (0.0035). % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/recalibrated_metrics.json]]\nThe stacked variant tracks it on the synthetic family (0.0707) and gene expression (0.0278) but is smaller on deforestation and protein disorder; the smooth label-free basis is close to PPI++ on the synthetic family (0.0034), where the nonlinearity is the exponential regime of the search generator. % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/stacked_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/label_free_basis_metrics.json]]\nSearch-pool pooled coverage was 0.943 for the recalibrated and the stacked variants and 0.950 for the basis variant, with minimum family coverage 0.933, 0.917 and 0.933 respectively. % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/recalibrated_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/stacked_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/label_free_basis_metrics.json]]\nThese are single evaluations without uncertainty estimates, and the variants differ in several components at once (recalibrator class, variance estimator, selection rule), so the contrast cannot attribute the difference to any one component. The more elaborate stack did not beat the simpler recalibrated interval on the aggregate, which is consistent with the headroom analysis: when the ceiling is small, richer calibrators have little to gain and pay an estimation cost.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/search_variant_contrast.pdf}\n\\caption{Design-space contrast on the search pools: log width ratio against PPI++ for three model variants, five families, one evaluation per variant. Separate from, and not comparable in scale to, the sealed final results.}\n\\label{fig:search}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:discussion}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} With fixed inputs, an interval narrower than PPI++ exists in the families we tested, but the average margin is about 2\\% in width and the margin depends strongly on the pool: close to zero where the predictor is already well calibrated and linear (census insurance, census education), a few percent where the PPI++ clip binds (census income) or where the calibration is nonlinear (synthetic regime). The population analysis predicts the sign and the rough size of these margins for census income and synthetic data, and predicts none for the plankton pool, whose predictor takes two values and whose unclipped optimal weight lies inside $[0,1]$.\n\n\\paragraph{What it does not support.} The evidence does not show that the narrowing is free: coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below 0.95, and the baselines' coverage is unknown. The observed gain exceeds the ceiling in four of five families, so a part of it is not explained by recalibration, and plankton in particular cannot be attributed to a nonlinear calibration. The paper does not claim superiority beyond the pools evaluated, validity under the selection rule beyond the empirical gate, or generalization to other estimands or to covariate shift.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Estimand: only the population mean at $\\alpha=0.05$. (2) Sample sizes: $n=500$ on real pools and $n\\in\\{200,500\\}$ on synthetic draws; no sweep over $n$ or $N$, although the theory predicts that small $n$ erodes the gain. (3) Single sealed run, no confidence intervals on width ratios, no per-episode widths or per-family coverage beyond the minimum in the artifacts, and no controlled ablation of the recalibrator, the second control variate, the Student-$t$ quantile or the selection rule. The mechanisms discussed above are therefore hypotheses. (4) The ceiling is a population-level asymptotic quantity computed with the evaluator's finite pools as the population; it is a diagnostic, not a bound on a finite draw. (5) The truth for real pools is the pool mean, with draws from the pool with replacement, so the evaluation checks inference for that finite population rather than for the original scientific population. (6) The evaluator's diagnostics for the candidate are marked as unverified. (7) The selection rule makes the interval data-dependent and the 1\\% margin has no theoretical guarantee. (8) Stratified PPI is implemented but has no reported metrics and is not compared numerically.\n\n\\paragraph{Experiments that would test the hypotheses.} The mechanisms above can be tested with controlled comparisons that we did not run: (i) PPI++ with the weight clip removed, to separate the clipping component from nonlinear calibration; (ii) the recalibrated interval with the fallback disabled and with a fixed choice of recalibrator, to separate selection effects from the second control variate; (iii) the Student-$t$ quantile replaced by the normal quantile; (iv) coverage and width of every baseline, computed on the same draws and reported per family; and (v) a grid over $n$ and $N$ on the real pools, where the theory predicts a gain that shrinks with $n$ (estimation cost of order $p/n$) and with $r$. Until these are available, the decomposition in Section~\\ref{sec:headroomcheck} should be read as a diagnostic of what is possible, not as an attribution of what the evaluated interval does.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} As a population-level diagnostic, for census income about 84\\% of the computed ceiling (0.0179 of 0.0213) is attributable to the weight clip, whereas recalibration of a visibly nonlinear $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ accounts for the ceiling in the synthetic nonlinear regime. Whether the clip-attributable part can be attained by an unclipped PPI++ interval, and at what coverage cost, was not tested here.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWith labels, unlabeled data and predictor held fixed, a cross-fitted recalibration added as a second control variate to PPI++ shortened the mean interval by 2.2\\% on average in a sealed evaluation, with reductions between 0.2\\% and 4.0\\% by family, and 6.8\\% and 30\\% relative to PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and the labeled-only interval. A closed-form headroom analysis explains why the gain is small and regime-dependent: it is bounded by the weight clip and by the nonlinearity of $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$, and is zero for the plankton pool, whose two-valued predictor has an unclipped optimal weight inside $[0,1]$. It also shows that the observed gain is larger than that ceiling in four of five families, and coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below the nominal 0.95, although the evaluator's tests did not reject. We read the result as conditional support for a small gain, not as evidence that PPI++ intervals can be shortened for free. The most useful next experiments are a coverage and width comparison of an unclipped PPI++ weight and each recalibration component in isolation, per-episode width and coverage reporting for all baselines, and a sweep over $n$.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n39:\\begin{abstract}\n41:\\end{abstract}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nPrediction-powered inference (PPI) combines a small labeled sample, a large unlabeled sample and a fixed pretrained predictor into a valid confidence interval for a population mean. We ask how much further such an interval can be shortened when labels, unlabeled data and predictor are all held fixed. Writing PPI++ as a control-variate estimator, we show that the attainable gain is governed by the gap between the squared correlation of $Y$ with $\\Yhat$ and the correlation ratio of $Y$ given $\\Yhat$, plus a clipping effect on the tuning weight. We evaluate a cross-fitted recalibration (isotonic or binned) added as a second control variate, with a fallback to PPI++, on a sealed evaluation of four real pools and known-truth synthetic regimes. The interval is 2.2\\% narrower than PPI++ (geometric mean over five families), 6.8\\% narrower than PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and 30\\% narrower than the labeled-only interval; per-family reductions range from 0.2\\% to 4.0\\%. Pooled coverage is 0.934 (lowest family 0.91), below the nominal 0.95 although not rejected by the evaluator's exact tests, and the observed narrowing exceeds our population-level ceiling in four of five families. The evidence supports a small, regime-dependent gain; it does not show that the gain is free of a coverage cost.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nIn ecological remote sensing, galaxy morphology and census analysis, a scientist often holds three things: a small set of gold-standard labels, a large set of records without labels, and a pretrained predictor that cannot be modified. Prediction-powered inference \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PredictionpoweredI} and its power-tuned form PPI++ \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PPIEP} use the predictor on both samples to produce confidence intervals that remain valid for any predictor and are shorter than the labeled-only interval when the predictor is informative.\n\nWe study a deliberately narrow question. \\emph{With the labeled set, the unlabeled set and the predictor all fixed, can a valid mean interval be shortened below PPI++? By how much, and under which conditions does it help or fail?} Fixing the inputs excludes active labeling \\citep{Zrnic2024ActiveSI}, retraining the predictor, and adding covariates or data; the only remaining freedom is how $(Y,\\Yhat)$ pairs and unlabeled predictions are turned into an interval. The labeled-only data and the predictions are all that any such interval can use.\n\nOur route is a nested one. PPI++ is a control-variate estimator whose control variate is $\\Yhat$ with a scalar weight clipped to $[0,1]$. We add a second control variate $g(\\Yhat)$, a recalibration of the prediction fitted on the labeled data by cross-fitting, and let a variance-minimizing weight vector combine the two. A selection rule falls back to PPI++ unless the recalibrated interval is at least 1\\% shorter. The question then reduces to how much variance the second control variate can remove.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Headroom analysis.} For control-variate mean intervals with fixed inputs we give the asymptotic variance in closed form (Section~\\ref{sec:theory}). Relative to PPI++ the gain depends on two quantities only: the clipping of the tuning weight, and the gap $\\eta^2-\\rho^2$ between the correlation ratio of $Y$ on $\\Yhat$ and the squared Pearson correlation. We list the conditions under which the gain is zero; a two-valued predictor removes the nonlinearity term but not necessarily the clipping term.\n\\item \\textbf{A recalibrated PPI interval} with isotonic and binned recalibrators, cross-fitting, Student-$t$ critical values and a 1\\%-margin fallback to PPI++ (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n\\item \\textbf{A sealed evaluation} on four real pools and four known-truth synthetic regimes, scored against classical, PPI ($\\lambda{=}1$) and PPI++ intervals, with the width gain, the coverage evidence and the population-level ceiling reported side by side (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\\item \\textbf{A calibrated conclusion.} The recalibrated interval is narrower than PPI++ by 2.2\\% on average, but the average exceeds the population-level ceiling and coverage point estimates sit below 0.95. We state precisely what the evidence does and does not support (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}).\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/overview_setting_and_method.png}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"99283740-f224-4937-b18b-b055c501dd53","uuid":"7c3ebf69-30d9-4553-80cb-8fae1032740b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:27:44.167Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\midrule\naggregate & 0.0220 & 2.2\\% & 0.0129 & 0.0036 & 0.0091 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_vs_ppi_pp]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_linear_vs_ppi_pp]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Does the headroom analysis explain the gains?}\\label{sec:headroomcheck}\nWe evaluated Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling} on each pool, using $\\rho^2$ and an isotonic estimate of $\\eta^2$ on the full pool, $n=500$, $N=20000$, and the evaluator's PPI++ weight clip (for synthetic regimes, large-sample moments of the generator and the evaluator's episode plan). The population ceiling is not a bound on a single finite draw, and it excludes the estimation cost of $g$.\n\n\\emph{Where the ceiling is positive, it is small.} The ceiling against PPI++ is 0.0213 for census income, 0.0008 and 0.0009 for the two other census pools, and 0.0416 for the synthetic mixture; the aggregate is 0.0129. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_vs_ppi_pp]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_aggregate_vs_ppi_pp]]\nFor census income, the unclipped optimal weight computed from the pool is 1.34 (the clipped weight equals 1), and 0.0179 of the 0.0213 ceiling is reachable by an unclipped linear weight on $\\Yhat$ alone; the pool's $\\eta^2$ and $\\rho^2$ differ by only 0.0044. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_linear_vs_ppi_pp]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real.census_income]]\nSo for this family the predicted gain is mostly a consequence of the clip on the PPI++ weight, not of nonlinear recalibration. For the synthetic family, the entire ceiling comes from the nonlinear-miscalibrated regime ($\\eta^2=0.71$ against $\\rho^2=0.48$), and the observed 0.0413 matches the ceiling 0.0416 within 0.0003. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#synthetic_regimes]]\n\n\\emph{Where the observed gain exceeds the ceiling.} In four of five families the observed narrowing is larger than the ceiling (Table~\\ref{tab:final}). The plankton pool is the clearest case: the predictor takes only two values, so $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is affine in $\\Yhat$, the unclipped optimal weight (0.841) lies inside $[0,1]$, the ceiling is zero, yet the observed narrowing is 0.0359 (3.5\\%). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real.plankton]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_plankton]]\nThe aggregate observed value 0.0220 exceeds the aggregate ceiling 0.0129 by 0.0091. The same holds against PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ (0.0701 observed, 0.0661 ceiling) and against the labeled-only interval (0.3581 observed, 0.3498 ceiling). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_aggregate_vs_ppi_lam1]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_aggregate_vs_classical]]\nAsymptotic variance reduction from recalibration cannot account for this part. We did not run a diagnostic that isolates its source. Candidate explanations, none tested here, are (a) finite-sample optimism of an interval whose weight and residual variance are estimated on the same labeled data, (b) selection of the smaller of three estimated widths, which favors downward noise in the estimated standard error, and (c) differences in finite-sample variance estimation between the evaluated implementation and the reference baseline. Explanations (a) and (b) would also lower coverage and are consistent with the coverage shortfall of Section~\\ref{sec:validity}, but the available artifacts do not allow us to establish that link. For the two census pools with near-zero ceilings, the excess is 0.0009 and 0.0014; with no per-episode widths in the artifacts, we cannot tell whether it is distinguishable from pairing noise.\n\n\\emph{Regime-level headroom is much larger than the family-level average.} Within the nonlinear-miscalibrated synthetic regime alone, the same formula gives an oracle narrowing over an unclipped linear weight of 0.286 in log width for $(n,N)=(200,20000)$ and 0.178 for $(500,2000)$ (Table~\\ref{tab:rsens}), i.e.\\ roughly 25\\% to 16\\% shorter intervals; the gain falls as $r=n/N$ grows because the unlabeled sample then carries less of the variance. The family-level ceiling of 0.0416 is much smaller because three of the four regimes have no headroom and the evaluator's ratio of summed widths weights the nonlinear regime by its (smallest) interval width. For census income the same calculation gives at most 0.0034 over an unclipped linear weight, whatever $r$. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_recal_over_linear_by_cell]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Oracle recalibration gain over an unclipped linear weight on $\\hat Y$ (log width ratio, Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling}) by sample-size cell, for the one synthetic regime with nonlinear calibration and for the census-income pool (derived).}\n\\label{tab:rsens}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\n & $(200,20000)$ & $(500,20000)$ & $(200,2000)$ & $(500,2000)$ \\\\\n$r=n/N$ & 0.010 & 0.025 & 0.100 & 0.250 \\\\\n\\midrule\nsynthetic, nonlinear regime & 0.2855 & 0.2750 & 0.2323 & 0.1776 \\\\\ncensus income & 0.0034 & 0.0034 & 0.0030 & 0.0025 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_recal_over_linear_by_cell]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Stability across evaluator partitions}\nThe evaluator also reports the aggregate score separately for five partitions of the episodes, defined by the candidate reset seed: 0.0247, 0.0220, 0.0203, 0.0213 and 0.0235, all positive. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\nThese values are a spread, not a confidence interval: the data draws do not depend on the reset seed, and because of the evaluator's episode ordering each partition contains 76 episodes of one family and six of each other family, so partitions differ in composition (Supplement~\\ref{sec:si-partition}). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#partition_composition_main_episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Design-space contrast on the search pools}\\label{sec:search}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:search} compares the three model variants, each evaluated once on the search pools. Their aggregate scores against PPI++ are 0.0271 (recalibrated isotonic/binned), 0.0223 (stacked calibration) and 0.0046 (label-free basis with PRESS); all three were scored and passed the evaluator's validity gate. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_01]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_02]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_03]]\nThe recalibrated variant is largest on the synthetic family (0.0757) and gene expression (0.0290), and smallest on galaxy morphology (0.0035). % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/recalibrated_metrics.json]]\nThe stacked variant tracks it on the synthetic family (0.0707) and gene expression (0.0278) but is smaller on deforestation and protein disorder; the smooth label-free basis is close to PPI++ on the synthetic family (0.0034), where the nonlinearity is the exponential regime of the search generator. % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/stacked_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/label_free_basis_metrics.json]]\nSearch-pool pooled coverage was 0.943 for the recalibrated and the stacked variants and 0.950 for the basis variant, with minimum family coverage 0.933, 0.917 and 0.933 respectively. % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/recalibrated_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/stacked_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/label_free_basis_metrics.json]]\nThese are single evaluations without uncertainty estimates, and the variants differ in several components at once (recalibrator class, variance estimator, selection rule), so the contrast cannot attribute the difference to any one component. The more elaborate stack did not beat the simpler recalibrated interval on the aggregate, which is consistent with the headroom analysis: when the ceiling is small, richer calibrators have little to gain and pay an estimation cost.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/search_variant_contrast.pdf}\n\\caption{Design-space contrast on the search pools: log width ratio against PPI++ for three model variants, five families, one evaluation per variant. Separate from, and not comparable in scale to, the sealed final results.}\n\\label{fig:search}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:discussion}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} With fixed inputs, an interval narrower than PPI++ exists in the families we tested, but the average margin is about 2\\% in width and the margin depends strongly on the pool: close to zero where the predictor is already well calibrated and linear (census insurance, census education), a few percent where the PPI++ clip binds (census income) or where the calibration is nonlinear (synthetic regime). The population analysis predicts the sign and the rough size of these margins for census income and synthetic data, and predicts none for the plankton pool, whose predictor takes two values and whose unclipped optimal weight lies inside $[0,1]$.\n\n\\paragraph{What it does not support.} The evidence does not show that the narrowing is free: coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below 0.95, and the baselines' coverage is unknown. The observed gain exceeds the ceiling in four of five families, so a part of it is not explained by recalibration, and plankton in particular cannot be attributed to a nonlinear calibration. The paper does not claim superiority beyond the pools evaluated, validity under the selection rule beyond the empirical gate, or generalization to other estimands or to covariate shift.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Estimand: only the population mean at $\\alpha=0.05$. (2) Sample sizes: $n=500$ on real pools and $n\\in\\{200,500\\}$ on synthetic draws; no sweep over $n$ or $N$, although the theory predicts that small $n$ erodes the gain. (3) Single sealed run, no confidence intervals on width ratios, no per-episode widths or per-family coverage beyond the minimum in the artifacts, and no controlled ablation of the recalibrator, the second control variate, the Student-$t$ quantile or the selection rule. The mechanisms discussed above are therefore hypotheses. (4) The ceiling is a population-level asymptotic quantity computed with the evaluator's finite pools as the population; it is a diagnostic, not a bound on a finite draw. (5) The truth for real pools is the pool mean, with draws from the pool with replacement, so the evaluation checks inference for that finite population rather than for the original scientific population. (6) The evaluator's diagnostics for the candidate are marked as unverified. (7) The selection rule makes the interval data-dependent and the 1\\% margin has no theoretical guarantee. (8) Stratified PPI is implemented but has no reported metrics and is not compared numerically.\n\n\\paragraph{Experiments that would test the hypotheses.} The mechanisms above can be tested with controlled comparisons that we did not run: (i) PPI++ with the weight clip removed, to separate the clipping component from nonlinear calibration; (ii) the recalibrated interval with the fallback disabled and with a fixed choice of recalibrator, to separate selection effects from the second control variate; (iii) the Student-$t$ quantile replaced by the normal quantile; (iv) coverage and width of every baseline, computed on the same draws and reported per family; and (v) a grid over $n$ and $N$ on the real pools, where the theory predicts a gain that shrinks with $n$ (estimation cost of order $p/n$) and with $r$. Until these are available, the decomposition in Section~\\ref{sec:headroomcheck} should be read as a diagnostic of what is possible, not as an attribution of what the evaluated interval does.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} As a population-level diagnostic, for census income about 84\\% of the computed ceiling (0.0179 of 0.0213) is attributable to the weight clip, whereas recalibration of a visibly nonlinear $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ accounts for the ceiling in the synthetic nonlinear regime. Whether the clip-attributable part can be attained by an unclipped PPI++ interval, and at what coverage cost, was not tested here.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWith labels, unlabeled data and predictor held fixed, a cross-fitted recalibration added as a second control variate to PPI++ shortened the mean interval by 2.2\\% on average in a sealed evaluation, with reductions between 0.2\\% and 4.0\\% by family, and 6.8\\% and 30\\% relative to PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and the labeled-only interval. A closed-form headroom analysis explains why the gain is small and regime-dependent: it is bounded by the weight clip and by the nonlinearity of $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$, and is zero for the plankton pool, whose two-valued predictor has an unclipped optimal weight inside $[0,1]$. It also shows that the observed gain is larger than that ceiling in four of five families, and coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below the nominal 0.95, although the evaluator's tests did not reject. We read the result as conditional support for a small gain, not as evidence that PPI++ intervals can be shortened for free. The most useful next experiments are a coverage and width comparison of an unclipped PPI++ weight and each recalibration component in isolation, per-episode width and coverage reporting for all baselines, and a sweep over $n$.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n39:\\begin{abstract}\n41:\\end{abstract}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nPrediction-powered inference (PPI) combines a small labeled sample, a large unlabeled sample and a fixed pretrained predictor into a valid confidence interval for a population mean. We ask how much further such an interval can be shortened when labels, unlabeled data and predictor are all held fixed. Writing PPI++ as a control-variate estimator, we show that the attainable gain is governed by the gap between the squared correlation of $Y$ with $\\Yhat$ and the correlation ratio of $Y$ given $\\Yhat$, plus a clipping effect on the tuning weight. We evaluate a cross-fitted recalibration (isotonic or binned) added as a second control variate, with a fallback to PPI++, on a sealed evaluation of four real pools and known-truth synthetic regimes. The interval is 2.2\\% narrower than PPI++ (geometric mean over five families), 6.8\\% narrower than PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and 30\\% narrower than the labeled-only interval; per-family reductions range from 0.2\\% to 4.0\\%. Pooled coverage is 0.934 (lowest family 0.91), below the nominal 0.95 although not rejected by the evaluator's exact tests, and the observed narrowing exceeds our population-level ceiling in four of five families. The evidence supports a small, regime-dependent gain; it does not show that the gain is free of a coverage cost.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nIn ecological remote sensing, galaxy morphology and census analysis, a scientist often holds three things: a small set of gold-standard labels, a large set of records without labels, and a pretrained predictor that cannot be modified. Prediction-powered inference \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PredictionpoweredI} and its power-tuned form PPI++ \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PPIEP} use the predictor on both samples to produce confidence intervals that remain valid for any predictor and are shorter than the labeled-only interval when the predictor is informative.\n\nWe study a deliberately narrow question. \\emph{With the labeled set, the unlabeled set and the predictor all fixed, can a valid mean interval be shortened below PPI++? By how much, and under which conditions does it help or fail?} Fixing the inputs excludes active labeling \\citep{Zrnic2024ActiveSI}, retraining the predictor, and adding covariates or data; the only remaining freedom is how $(Y,\\Yhat)$ pairs and unlabeled predictions are turned into an interval. The labeled-only data and the predictions are all that any such interval can use.\n\nOur route is a nested one. PPI++ is a control-variate estimator whose control variate is $\\Yhat$ with a scalar weight clipped to $[0,1]$. We add a second control variate $g(\\Yhat)$, a recalibration of the prediction fitted on the labeled data by cross-fitting, and let a variance-minimizing weight vector combine the two. A selection rule falls back to PPI++ unless the recalibrated interval is at least 1\\% shorter. The question then reduces to how much variance the second control variate can remove.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Headroom analysis.} For control-variate mean intervals with fixed inputs we give the asymptotic variance in closed form (Section~\\ref{sec:theory}). Relative to PPI++ the gain depends on two quantities only: the clipping of the tuning weight, and the gap $\\eta^2-\\rho^2$ between the correlation ratio of $Y$ on $\\Yhat$ and the squared Pearson correlation. We list the conditions under which the gain is zero; a two-valued predictor removes the nonlinearity term but not necessarily the clipping term.\n\\item \\textbf{A recalibrated PPI interval} with isotonic and binned recalibrators, cross-fitting, Student-$t$ critical values and a 1\\%-margin fallback to PPI++ (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n\\item \\textbf{A sealed evaluation} on four real pools and four known-truth synthetic regimes, scored against classical, PPI ($\\lambda{=}1$) and PPI++ intervals, with the width gain, the coverage evidence and the population-level ceiling reported side by side (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\\item \\textbf{A calibrated conclusion.} The recalibrated interval is narrower than PPI++ by 2.2\\% on average, but the average exceeds the population-level ceiling and coverage point estimates sit below 0.95. We state precisely what the evidence does and does not support (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}).\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/overview_setting_and_method.png}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
